const M=[
["Ionic Bonding & Lattice Energy","Ionic bonding is attraction between oppositely charged ions. <div class=call><b>AP rule:</b> stronger attraction comes from larger ionic charges and smaller distance. Compare charge first, then ion size.</div>MgO (2+/2−) has stronger attractions than NaCl (1+/1−). With equal charges, smaller ions attract more strongly. Ionic solids conduct when molten or dissolved because ions move; solids conduct poorly because ions are fixed.",[["What most increases ionic attraction?","Larger ionic charges","Larger separation","Larger ions","More neutrons","<b>Why:</b> Opposite electrical charges attract. Increasing the size of the charges makes that pull stronger. Larger separation or larger ions usually place the charges farther apart, which weakens attraction; neutron count is not the key factor."],["Which has stronger lattice attraction?","MgO","NaCl","KBr","CsI","<b>Why:</b> MgO contains Mg²⁺ and O²⁻, so its charge product is much larger than the ±1 ions in the other choices. Larger charges create stronger electrostatic attraction."],["Why does molten NaCl conduct?","Ions can move","Protons move","It becomes metallic","Cl disappears","<b>Why:</b> Melting frees Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions to move and carry charge. Protons remain in nuclei, NaCl does not become a metal, and chlorine does not disappear."],["Equal charges: stronger attraction favors…","Smaller ions","Larger ions","Higher mass only","More isotopes","<b>Why:</b> With charge held constant, smaller ions let opposite charges get closer together. Closer charges attract more strongly. Mass and isotope count are not the deciding variables."],["Why does solid NaCl conduct poorly?","Ions are fixed","No charges exist","Na is nonmetal","Cl has no electrons","<b>Why:</b> Solid NaCl absolutely contains charged Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions, but they are locked into the crystal lattice and cannot flow through the material to carry current."],["Typical ionic bonding is between…","metal and nonmetal","two noble gases","identical nonmetals","neutral molecules","<b>Why:</b> Metals tend to lose electrons and form positive ions; nonmetals tend to gain electrons and form negative ions. Those opposite ions attract."]]],
["Metallic, Covalent & Network","Metals have delocalized valence electrons; their mobility explains conductivity and their bonding helps explain malleability. Covalent bonds share electron density. Molecular substances contain separate molecules; network covalent solids contain an extended web of bonds.<div class=call>Structure predicts properties: diamond is a rigid covalent network. Graphite has bonded sheets and delocalized electrons, allowing conductivity.</div>",[["Metal conductivity comes from…","mobile delocalized electrons","free protons","moving nuclei","dissolved ions"],["Diamond is…","network covalent","ionic","metallic","monatomic gas"],["Graphite conducts because of…","delocalized electrons","mobile protons","metal atoms","free neutrons"],["Metal malleability fits…","layers shift while electrons maintain attraction","all bonds break","electrons vanish","ions dissolve"],["Molecular covalent matter has…","discrete molecules","only cations","endless networks always","metal ions"],["Network solids often melt high because…","many strong bonds must be disrupted","they have free ions","they lack electrons","all are gases"]]],
["Bond Order, Sigma & Pi","Single, double and triple bonds have orders 1, 2 and 3. Higher bond order generally means <b>shorter and stronger</b>. The first bond is sigma (σ), from head-on overlap; additional bonds are pi (π), from side-by-side overlap.<div class=call>Single = 1σ. Double = 1σ + 1π. Triple = 1σ + 2π. Pi overlap restricts rotation.</div>",[["A triple bond contains…","1 σ and 2 π","3 σ","2 σ and 1 π","3 π"],["As bond order rises, length generally…","decreases","increases","never changes","becomes zero"],["Normally strongest is…","triple","double","single","all equal"],["The first bond is…","sigma","pi","ionic","hydrogen"],["Double-bond rotation is restricted because it…","disrupts π overlap","removes nuclei","creates ions","removes σ electrons"],["A double bond contains…","1 σ and 1 π","2 σ","2 π","1 π only"]]],
["Potential Energy & Bond Length","Far-apart atoms interact weakly. As they approach, attraction lowers potential energy until the <b>equilibrium bond length</b>, the curve minimum. Too close, repulsions make energy rise steeply.<div class=call>Minimum = equilibrium distance. A deeper energy well means a stronger bond because more energy is needed to separate atoms.</div>",[["Curve minimum represents…","equilibrium bond length","maximum repulsion","zero electrons","nuclear overlap"],["Deeper well means…","stronger bond","weaker bond","no attraction","larger atom only"],["Very short distance raises energy because…","repulsion dominates","charges vanish","gravity dominates","atoms become neutrons"],["Breaking a bond requires…","energy input","proton loss","zero energy","metal formation"],["Very far apart gives…","weak interaction","maximum overlap","ionic lattice","fusion"],["A stronger bond has…","deeper energy well","no minimum","only repulsion","no electrons"]]]
];
M.push(
["Conductivity & Dissociation","Conductivity requires <b>mobile charged particles</b>. Metals use mobile electrons. Ionic solids contain charges but their ions are locked in place. Many ionic compounds dissociate in water into mobile hydrated ions; molecular nonelectrolytes can dissolve yet remain neutral.<div class=ex>NaCl(aq) → Na⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq). Water separates existing ions.</div>",[["Which conducts well?","NaCl(aq)","neutral sugar solution","oil","liquid with no mobile charge"],["Current in salt water is carried by…","mobile ions","neutrons","stationary nuclei","bonds"],["Ionic solids fail to conduct because…","ions cannot move","no charges exist","electrons lack mass","they are gases"],["CaCl₂ dissociates to…","Ca²⁺ + 2Cl⁻","Ca⁺ + Cl⁻","Ca²⁻ + 2Cl⁺","neutral atoms"],["Nonelectrolytes conduct poorly because of…","few mobile charges","extra protons","metal atoms","no molecules"],["Evidence of mobile ions is…","electrical conductivity","mass","transparency","volume"]]],
["Lewis Structures","Lewis structures account for valence electrons. Use: <b>count → skeleton → single bonds → terminal octets → leftovers on center → multiple bonds if needed.</b><div class=call>Negative charge adds electrons; positive charge subtracts them. Hydrogen normally wants 2; many second-period atoms seek 8.</div><div class=ex>CO₂ has 16 valence electrons. O=C=O gives carbon an octet and each oxygen two lone pairs.</div>",[["Valence electrons in CO₂?","16","12","18","24"],["For a −1 ion, count by…","adding one electron","subtracting one","adding proton","ignoring charge"],["Hydrogen normally seeks…","2 electrons","8","12","0"],["After skeleton and single bonds…","complete terminal octets","erase bonds","remove electrons","triple every bond"],["A Lewis bond line means…","shared electron pair","proton","neutron","crystal"],["Recount electrons to…","verify total valence electrons","find mass","count neutrons","change elements"]]],
["Resonance, Formal Charge & Exceptions","Resonance contributors keep atom connectivity but differ in electron placement. The real species is a <b>resonance hybrid</b>. Formal charge = valence − nonbonding − ½(bonding electrons). Favor reasonable structures with minimized formal-charge magnitude.<div class=call>Equivalent resonance can produce fractional average bond orders. Exceptions include electron-deficient B compounds, odd-electron species, and expanded shells for some period-3-and-beyond centers.</div>",[["Resonance contributors differ in…","electron placement","atom identity","proton count","formula"],["Formal charge formula is…","valence − nonbonding − ½ bonding","mass − protons","neutrons + bonds","valence + bonding"],["Actual resonance species is…","a hybrid","two compounds switching","always ionic","electron-free"],["Often electron-deficient element?","boron","neon","argon","helium"],["Equivalent resonance can produce…","intermediate bond orders","different atomic numbers","no electron density","unrelated formulas"],["Preferred structures usually have…","smaller formal-charge magnitudes","largest charges","no lone pairs","wrong totals"]]],
["VSEPR & Molecular Geometry","VSEPR minimizes electron-domain repulsions. Single, double and triple bonds each count as <b>one domain</b>; each lone pair is one domain. 2 = linear (180°); 3 = trigonal planar (120°); 4 = tetrahedral (109.5°); 5 = trigonal bipyramidal; 6 = octahedral.<div class=call>Molecular geometry names atom positions, not lone pairs. AX₄ tetrahedral; AX₃E trigonal pyramidal; AX₂E₂ bent. With 5 domains, lone pairs prefer equatorial sites: AX₄E seesaw, AX₃E₂ T-shaped, AX₂E₃ linear.</div>",[["AX₄ is…","tetrahedral","trigonal planar","linear","octahedral"],["AX₃E is…","trigonal pyramidal","trigonal planar","T-shaped","linear"],["AX₂E₂ is…","bent","linear","tetrahedral","square planar"],["Five-domain electron geometry is…","trigonal bipyramidal","tetrahedral","octahedral","trigonal planar"],["Six-domain electron geometry is…","octahedral","tetrahedral","linear","trigonal bipyramidal"],["Trigonal-bipyramidal lone pairs prefer…","equatorial","axial always","the nucleus","outside molecule"]]]
);
M.push(
["Intermolecular Forces: Why Molecules Stick","Now we move from bonds <i>inside</i> a molecule to attractions <i>between</i> molecules. Every particle has London dispersion forces because electron clouds fluctuate. Polar molecules also attract through dipole–dipole forces. When H is bonded directly to N, O, or F, especially strong hydrogen bonding can occur.<div class=call><b>Beginner picture:</b> intramolecular bonds build the molecule; intermolecular forces make neighboring molecules cling to one another. Stronger attractions usually mean more energy is needed to separate particles, so boiling points tend to rise.</div><div class=ex>Water has O–H bonds and can hydrogen-bond strongly. Methane is nonpolar and relies mainly on dispersion forces.</div>",[["Which force exists in every atom and molecule?","London dispersion","Hydrogen bonding only","Ionic bonding","Metallic bonding"],["Hydrogen bonding commonly requires H directly bonded to…","N, O, or F","Na, K, or Li","any noble gas","only carbon"],["Stronger intermolecular attractions usually cause…","higher boiling point","lower particle attraction","fewer electrons always","ionic bonding"],["Dipole–dipole attraction occurs between…","polar molecules","only metals","only ions in crystals","neutrons"],["Intermolecular means forces…","between separate particles","inside a nucleus","only within one covalent bond","between protons only"],["Large electron clouds often have stronger dispersion because they are…","more polarizable","always ionic","smaller","electron-free"]]],
["States of Matter & Phase Changes","Solids, liquids, and gases differ in particle arrangement, motion, and attraction. Solid particles vibrate around fixed positions. Liquid particles remain close but can flow past one another. Gas particles are far apart and move freely.<div class=call>Heating does not make particles themselves grow. It increases particle motion. During a phase change, added energy mainly changes particle attractions rather than temperature.</div><div class=ex>Melting loosens the organized solid structure. Vaporization requires enough energy for particles to separate much more dramatically.</div>",[["Gas particles are generally…","far apart and freely moving","locked in place","closer than solid particles always","motionless"],["In a liquid, particles…","stay close but can flow","are fixed in a lattice","have no attractions","are infinitely separated"],["During boiling at constant pressure, added heat mainly…","overcomes attractions","creates new elements","increases proton count","destroys electrons"],["A solid is characterized by particles that…","vibrate around fixed positions","travel independently across the container","have no motion at all","must be ionic"],["Vaporization changes a liquid into…","a gas","a solid","a plasma necessarily","an ion"],["Stronger intermolecular forces generally require…","more energy for vaporization","less energy for separation","no energy","fewer particles"]]],
["Gases: Pressure, Volume & Temperature","Gas behavior becomes easier when you imagine many tiny particles colliding with container walls. Pressure comes from those collisions. The ideal gas law is <b>PV = nRT</b>: pressure × volume equals moles × gas constant × temperature in kelvin.<div class=call><b>Middle-school bridge:</b> squeeze the same gas into a smaller box and wall collisions become more frequent, so pressure rises. Heat a gas and particles move faster.</div><div class=ex>If n and T stay constant, decreasing volume causes pressure to increase. Always use kelvin for gas-law temperature.</div>",[["Gas pressure results mainly from…","particle collisions with container walls","gravity only","chemical bonds to the wall","neutrons leaving atoms"],["At constant n and T, decreasing volume makes pressure…","increase","decrease","become zero","stay necessarily identical"],["The ideal gas law is…","PV = nRT","E = mc²","F = ma","pH = pKa"],["Gas-law temperature should normally be in…","kelvin","degrees Fahrenheit","any arbitrary scale","meters"],["Heating a gas generally makes particles…","move faster","stop moving","lose all mass","become solids"],["If volume increases at constant n and T, pressure tends to…","decrease","increase","triple always","become negative"]]],
["Solutions, Solubility & Like Dissolves Like","A solution is a uniform mixture. The <b>solvent</b> does the dissolving and the <b>solute</b> is dissolved. Whether something dissolves depends on whether new solute–solvent attractions can replace the attractions being disrupted.<div class=call><b>Useful rule:</b> 'like dissolves like.' Polar/ionic substances tend to interact well with polar solvents; nonpolar substances tend to mix better with nonpolar solvents.</div><div class=ex>Water is polar, so it hydrates ions effectively. Oil is mostly nonpolar, so oil and water separate.</div>",[["In salt water, water is usually the…","solvent","solute","precipitate","ion"],["The substance being dissolved is the…","solute","solvent","container","catalyst"],["A useful solubility rule is…","like dissolves like","all solids dissolve in water","nonpolar always dissolves ionic","temperature never matters"],["Water interacts well with many ions because water is…","polar","a metal","nonpolar","electron-free"],["Oil and water separate largely because…","their intermolecular interactions differ","water has no electrons","oil is ionic","both are metals"],["A solution is best described as…","a homogeneous mixture","always a pure element","always a solid","a nuclear reaction"]]],
["Chemical Reactions & Stoichiometry Foundations","A chemical reaction rearranges atoms into new combinations; atoms are conserved. A balanced equation is a particle recipe. Coefficients tell you relative numbers of particles and relative numbers of moles.<div class=call><b>Core bridge:</b> first balance the reaction, then use mole ratios. Never change subscripts to balance an equation because that changes the identity of the substance.</div><div class=ex>2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O means 2 mol H₂ react with 1 mol O₂ to make 2 mol H₂O.</div>",[["Balancing an equation changes…","coefficients","subscripts","element identities","atomic numbers"],["Atoms in an ordinary chemical reaction are…","rearranged and conserved","created from nothing","destroyed","converted into energy completely"],["In 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, H₂:O₂ mole ratio is…","2:1","1:2","1:1","4:1"],["Coefficients represent relative…","numbers of particles or moles","atomic numbers","neutron counts only","charges only"],["Why not change subscripts to balance?","It changes the chemical substance","It only changes font size","It changes temperature","It has no effect"],["Stoichiometry is mainly about…","quantitative relationships in reactions","naming orbitals only","measuring color only","nuclear decay only"]]]
);
M.push(
["Reaction Types & Net Ionic Equations","Reactions become easier when you learn to recognize patterns. Common AP categories include precipitation, acid–base, oxidation–reduction, and gas-forming reactions. In water, strong soluble electrolytes are written as ions.<div class=call><b>Net ionic equation:</b> remove spectator ions—ions that appear unchanged on both sides. What remains shows the particles actually undergoing chemical change.</div><div class=ex>Ag⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) → AgCl(s) is the net ionic equation for a silver chloride precipitation reaction.</div>",[["Spectator ions are ions that…","remain unchanged during the reaction","form the precipitate","always gain electrons","must be acids"],["A net ionic equation shows…","particles actually undergoing change","every spectator ion","only solvent molecules","atomic nuclei"],["Formation of an insoluble solid is called…","precipitation","vaporization","fusion","ionization energy"],["Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl(s) is a…","precipitation reaction","combustion reaction","nuclear reaction","gas law"],["Before canceling spectator ions, soluble strong electrolytes are often written as…","separate aqueous ions","solid atoms only","nuclei","electrons only"],["A spectator ion appears…","unchanged on both sides","only among products","only among reactants","inside the nucleus"]]],
["Acids, Bases & pH Foundations","Acids and bases can be introduced through proton transfer. A Brønsted–Lowry acid donates H⁺; a base accepts H⁺. In water, pH is a logarithmic measure related to hydronium concentration: <b>pH = −log[H₃O⁺]</b>.<div class=call><b>Beginner picture:</b> lower pH means more hydronium and greater acidity. A change of one pH unit represents a factor of ten in hydronium concentration.</div><div class=ex>A solution at pH 3 has ten times the hydronium concentration of a solution at pH 4.</div>",[["A Brønsted acid…","donates a proton","accepts a proton","donates neutrons","must contain metal"],["A Brønsted base…","accepts a proton","always donates H⁺","has no electrons","must be solid"],["Lower pH generally means…","greater hydronium concentration","less hydronium","more neutrons","lower temperature always"],["A one-unit pH change corresponds to a hydronium factor of…","10","2","1000","1"],["pH is defined as…","−log[H₃O⁺]","PV/nRT","q/m","mass/volume"],["Compared with pH 5, pH 3 has…","100 times the hydronium concentration","2 times","one-half","the same"]]],
["Thermochemistry: Heat, Energy & Enthalpy","Chemical systems exchange energy with their surroundings. <b>Exothermic</b> processes release heat to the surroundings; <b>endothermic</b> processes absorb heat. At constant pressure, enthalpy change ΔH tracks heat transfer.<div class=call><b>Sign convention:</b> exothermic ΔH is negative; endothermic ΔH is positive. Breaking bonds requires energy; forming bonds releases energy.</div><div class=ex>Combustion commonly releases heat, so the reaction has ΔH &lt; 0.</div>",[["An exothermic reaction…","releases heat","absorbs heat","has ΔH always positive","stops particle motion"],["For an endothermic process, ΔH is usually…","positive","negative","zero always","undefined"],["Breaking chemical bonds generally…","requires energy","releases energy","creates matter","removes protons"],["Forming bonds generally…","releases energy","requires energy only","destroys atoms","creates neutrons"],["Combustion is commonly…","exothermic","endothermic only","a phase change only","nuclear"],["At constant pressure, ΔH is associated with…","heat transfer","atomic number","electron count only","volume only"]]],
["Kinetics: How Fast Reactions Happen","Thermodynamics asks whether a process is energetically favorable; kinetics asks how fast it occurs. For reacting particles to change into products, they must collide with suitable orientation and enough energy to overcome the <b>activation energy</b> barrier.<div class=call>Higher temperature usually means more energetic collisions. Greater concentration can mean more frequent collisions. A catalyst provides a different pathway with lower activation energy but does not change the overall reaction ΔH.</div>",[["Kinetics studies…","reaction rates","only equilibrium position","atomic mass","nuclear charge"],["Activation energy is…","an energy barrier to reaction","energy stored in neutrons","the final temperature","always zero"],["Raising temperature usually makes reactions…","faster","slower","stop","become nuclear"],["Increasing reactant concentration often increases rate because…","collisions become more frequent","atoms lose nuclei","volume must become zero","electrons vanish"],["A catalyst works mainly by…","lowering activation energy","increasing ΔH","changing element identity","being consumed completely"],["A catalyst changes the reaction's overall ΔH by…","not changing it","making it always positive","making it zero","doubling it"]]],
["Equilibrium: Reactions Going Both Ways","Many reactions are reversible. At <b>dynamic equilibrium</b>, forward and reverse reactions continue, but their rates are equal, so macroscopic concentrations remain constant.<div class=call>Equilibrium does <b>not</b> mean equal amounts of reactants and products. It means equal forward and reverse rates. Le Châtelier's principle helps predict how an equilibrium responds to a disturbance.</div><div class=ex>If a reactant is added, the system often shifts in the direction that consumes some of that added reactant.</div>",[["At dynamic equilibrium, forward and reverse rates are…","equal","zero","unrelated","infinite"],["At equilibrium, reactant and product concentrations must be…","constant, not necessarily equal","exactly equal","zero","changing rapidly"],["Adding a reactant often shifts equilibrium toward…","products that consume it","more of the added reactant only","no reaction ever","nuclear products"],["Equilibrium reactions are often…","reversible","always irreversible","only solid-state","nuclear"],["Le Châtelier's principle predicts response to…","a disturbance in equilibrium conditions","atomic number","isotope identity","electron spin only"],["Dynamic equilibrium means reactions…","continue microscopically","have stopped completely","contain no molecules","must have equal concentrations"]]],
["Electrochemistry: Electrons Doing Work","Oxidation–reduction reactions transfer electrons. <b>Oxidation is loss</b> of electrons; <b>reduction is gain</b>. A galvanic (voltaic) cell uses a spontaneous redox reaction to produce electrical energy.<div class=call>Memory tool: OIL RIG — Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is Gain. Oxidation occurs at the anode; reduction occurs at the cathode.</div><div class=ex>When Zn becomes Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻, zinc is oxidized because it loses electrons.</div>",[["Oxidation means…","loss of electrons","gain of electrons","gain of neutrons","loss of protons always"],["Reduction means…","gain of electrons","loss of electrons","loss of mass","gain of neutrons only"],["Oxidation occurs at the…","anode","cathode","salt bridge only","voltmeter"],["Reduction occurs at the…","cathode","anode","wire only","solvent"],["Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻ is…","oxidation","reduction","neutralization","precipitation"],["A galvanic cell converts spontaneous redox chemistry into…","electrical energy","atomic mass","nuclear energy only","matter"]]]
);
const G={
0:`<div class="game challenge"><div class="challenge-title"><span>⚡</span><div><small>MISSION 01</small><h3>Build the Strongest Crystal</h3></div></div><p class="mission-text">Your goal: make the attraction score reach <b>9.00</b>. You control the ion charge and the distance between the ions.</p><div class="goalbar"><span>Target: 9.00</span><b id="goalStatus">Keep experimenting</b></div><div class="ions"><span id="ionA">+</span><span class="force" id="force">↔</span><span id="ionB">−</span></div><div class="control-card"><label>Charge on each ion <b id="chgVal">1</b><input id="chg" type="range" min="1" max="3" step="1" value="1"></label><div><button class="mini" id="chgDown">− charge</button><button class="mini" id="chgUp">+ charge</button></div><p class="hintline">Bigger charges act like stronger magnets.</p></div><div class="control-card"><label>Distance between ions <b id="distVal">3</b><input id="dist" type="range" min="1" max="5" step="1" value="3"></label><div><button class="mini" id="distDown">Move closer</button><button class="mini" id="distUp">Move farther</button></div><p class="hintline">Closer opposite charges pull harder.</p></div><div class="scorebox">Attraction score <strong id="readScore">0.11</strong></div><button class="go mission-check" id="latticeCheck">Check My Answer</button><button class="mini" id="latticeHint">I’m stuck — give me a hint</button><div id="latticeResult"></div></div>`,
2:`<div class="game challenge"><div class="challenge-title"><span>🔗</span><div><small>MISSION 02</small><h3>Build a Triple Bond</h3></div></div><p class="mission-text">Goal: choose the bond that contains <b>1 sigma (σ) + 2 pi (π)</b> bonds.</p><div class="bondviz"><span>●</span><span id="lines">—</span><span>●</span></div><div><button class="mini" data-bo="1">Single</button><button class="mini" data-bo="2">Double</button><button class="mini" data-bo="3">Triple</button></div><p id="bondread"></p><button class="mini" id="bondHint">Need a hint?</button><div id="bondResult"></div></div>`,
3:`<div class="game challenge"><div class="challenge-title"><span>🎢</span><div><small>MISSION 03</small><h3>Find the Bond Sweet Spot</h3></div></div><p class="mission-text">Goal: move the atoms until they reach the stable zone — <b>not too close and not too far</b>.</p><p>Separation: <b id="sepVal">75</b></p><input id="sep" type="range" min="1" max="100" step="1" value="75"><div><button class="mini" id="sepDown">Move closer</button><button class="mini" id="sepUp">Move farther</button></div><div class="atoms"><span>●</span><i id="gap"></i><span>●</span></div><div class="meter"><i id="energy"></i></div><p id="energyread"></p><button class="go" id="energyCheck">Check My Answer</button><button class="mini" id="energyHint">I’m stuck</button><div id="energyResult"></div></div>`,
7:`<div class="game challenge"><div class="challenge-title"><span>🧬</span><div><small>MISSION 04</small><h3>Shape Challenge: Water</h3></div></div><p class="mission-text">Water (H₂O) has <b>4 electron domains</b> around oxygen and <b>2 lone pairs</b>. Set those values to discover its molecular shape.</p><label>Electron domains <select id="domains"><option>2</option><option>3</option><option selected>4</option><option>5</option><option>6</option></select></label><label>Lone pairs <select id="lone"><option>0</option><option>1</option><option>2</option><option>3</option></select></label><div class="shape" id="shape"></div><p id="shaperead"></p><button class="go" id="shapeCheck">Check Water’s Shape</button><button class="mini" id="shapeHint">Need a hint?</button><div id="shapeResult"></div></div>`
};
function initGame(i){
if(i===0){let go=()=>{let q=+$("#chg").value,d=+$("#dist").value,f=(q*q)/(d*d);$("#chgVal").textContent=q;$("#distVal").textContent=d;$("#ionA").textContent=q+"+";$("#ionB").textContent=q+"−";$("#force").style.transform="scaleX("+Math.min(2.2,.7+f)+")";$("#readScore").textContent=f.toFixed(2);$("#goalStatus").textContent=f>=9?"TARGET REACHED!":"Target: 9.00"};$("#chg").oninput=$("#dist").oninput=go;$("#chgDown").onclick=()=>{$("#chg").value=Math.max(1,+$("#chg").value-1);go()};$("#chgUp").onclick=()=>{$("#chg").value=Math.min(3,+$("#chg").value+1);go()};$("#distDown").onclick=()=>{$("#dist").value=Math.max(1,+$("#dist").value-1);go()};$("#distUp").onclick=()=>{$("#dist").value=Math.min(5,+$("#dist").value+1);go()};$("#latticeHint").onclick=()=>{let q=+$("#chg").value,d=+$("#dist").value;$("#latticeResult").className="fb bot";$("#latticeResult").innerHTML=q<3?"<b>Hint 1:</b> Make the charges bigger. Bigger opposite charges pull on each other more strongly.":d>1?"<b>Hint 2:</b> Your charges are strong. Now bring the ions closer. Attraction gets stronger when opposite charges are nearer.":"You have the correct settings — press Check My Answer!"};$("#latticeCheck").onclick=()=>{let q=+$("#chg").value,d=+$("#dist").value,box=$("#latticeResult");if(q===3&&d===1){box.className="fb good";box.innerHTML="<b>Mission complete! 🎉</b><br>You made the strongest attraction: charge 3 and distance 1. <b>Why it works:</b> bigger opposite charges pull harder, and putting them closer makes the pull even stronger. This is the idea behind high lattice energy."}else{box.className="fb bad";box.innerHTML="<b>Not there yet — and that’s useful.</b><br>"+(q<3?"Your charges are still small. Increase them so the positive and negative ions pull harder. ":"Your charge is already as strong as possible. ")+(d>1?"The ions are still too far apart. Bring them closer so their attraction increases.":"Your distance is correct.")+"<br><b>Try again:</b> change one control, then recheck."}};go()}
if(i===2){let selected=1;let go=n=>{selected=n;let names=["","1 σ bond","1 σ + 1 π","1 σ + 2 π"];$("#lines").textContent=["","—","═","≡"][n];$("#bondread").innerHTML="<b>"+names[n]+"</b>";$("#bondResult").className="fb "+(n===3?"good":"bad");$("#bondResult").innerHTML=n===3?"<b>Mission complete! 🎉</b> A triple bond is 1σ + 2π. The first bond is always sigma; the two extra bonds are pi.":"<b>Not yet.</b> A "+(n===1?"single bond has only the first σ bond. You still need two additional π bonds.":"double bond has 1σ + 1π. You need one more π bond.")};document.querySelectorAll("[data-bo]").forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>go(+b.dataset.bo));$("#bondHint").onclick=()=>{$("#bondResult").className="fb bot";$("#bondResult").innerHTML="<b>Hint:</b> Start with one sigma bond. Each bond after the first is a pi bond. How many total bonds would give you two pi bonds?"};go(1)}
if(i===3){let go=()=>{let x=+$("#sep").value;$("#sepVal").textContent=x;$("#gap").style.width=(15+x*1.8)+"px";let e=Math.min(100,Math.abs(x-48)*2.5);$("#energy").style.width=e+"%";$("#energyread").textContent=x<35?"Too close: the atoms repel strongly.":x>62?"Too far: the atoms barely attract.":"You are in the stable zone."};$("#sep").oninput=go;$("#sepDown").onclick=()=>{$("#sep").value=Math.max(1,+$("#sep").value-5);go()};$("#sepUp").onclick=()=>{$("#sep").value=Math.min(100,+$("#sep").value+5);go()};$("#energyHint").onclick=()=>{let x=+$("#sep").value;$("#energyResult").className="fb bot";$("#energyResult").innerHTML=x>62?"<b>Hint:</b> The atoms are too far apart. Move them closer so attraction can hold them together.":x<35?"<b>Hint:</b> The atoms are crowded too close and repel. Move them farther apart.":"You are in the right zone — check your answer!"};$("#energyCheck").onclick=()=>{let x=+$("#sep").value,ok=x>=35&&x<=62;$("#energyResult").className="fb "+(ok?"good":"bad");$("#energyResult").innerHTML=ok?"<b>Mission complete! 🎉</b><br>You found the stable region. A bond is most stable at a middle distance where attraction is strong but repulsion has not become too large.":"<b>Not stable yet.</b><br>"+(x<35?"The atoms are too close, so repulsion is winning. Move farther apart.":"The atoms are too far apart, so attraction is too weak. Move closer.")};go()}
if(i===7){let go=()=>{let d=+$("#domains").value,l=+$("#lone").value,key=d+"-"+l,map={"2-0":"Linear • 180°","3-0":"Trigonal planar • 120°","3-1":"Bent","4-0":"Tetrahedral • 109.5°","4-1":"Trigonal pyramidal","4-2":"Bent","5-0":"Trigonal bipyramidal","5-1":"Seesaw","5-2":"T-shaped","5-3":"Linear","6-0":"Octahedral","6-1":"Square pyramidal","6-2":"Square planar"};let s=map[key]||"Invalid combination";$("#shape").textContent=s.split(" • ")[0];$("#shaperead").innerHTML="<b>"+s+"</b>"};$("#domains").onchange=$("#lone").onchange=go;$("#shapeHint").onclick=()=>{$("#shapeResult").className="fb bot";$("#shapeResult").innerHTML="<b>Hint:</b> Water’s oxygen has two O–H bonds plus two lone pairs. That makes 4 electron domains total and 2 lone pairs."};$("#shapeCheck").onclick=()=>{let d=+$("#domains").value,l=+$("#lone").value,ok=d===4&&l===2;$("#shapeResult").className="fb "+(ok?"good":"bad");$("#shapeResult").innerHTML=ok?"<b>Mission complete! 🎉 Water is bent.</b><br>The four electron groups spread out, but only two are bonds to H. The two lone pairs push on the bonds, giving H₂O its bent molecular shape.":"<b>Not quite.</b><br>"+(d!==4?"Water needs 4 total electron domains: two bonds plus two lone pairs. ":"Your domain count is right. ")+(l!==2?"Set lone pairs to 2. Those lone pairs are why water is bent instead of tetrahedral.":"")};go()}
}
const MISSIONS=[
{f:"H₂O",e:8,b:2,l:2,s:"Bent",why:"Oxygen has two O–H bonds and two lone pairs: four electron domains, AX₂E₂."},
{f:"NH₃",e:8,b:3,l:1,s:"Trigonal pyramidal",why:"Nitrogen has three N–H bonds and one lone pair: AX₃E."},
{f:"CO₂",e:16,b:4,l:0,s:"Linear",why:"Two C=O double bonds count as two electron domains around carbon, so CO₂ is linear."},
{f:"CH₄",e:8,b:4,l:0,s:"Tetrahedral",why:"Carbon has four bonding domains and no lone pairs: AX₄."},
{f:"BF₃",e:24,b:3,l:0,s:"Trigonal planar",why:"Boron has three bonding domains and no lone pairs; BF₃ is an octet exception."}
];
function missionGame(){let game={i:0,score:0,lives:3,streak:0};function draw(){let m=MISSIONS[game.i%MISSIONS.length];$("#main").innerHTML='<div class="game mission"><span class="pill">Molecule Mission</span><h2>Build '+m.f+'</h2><p>Configure the molecule correctly. You have '+game.lives+' ♥ • Score '+game.score+' • Streak '+game.streak+'</p><div class="molecule-orbit"><b>'+m.f+'</b><i></i><i></i><i></i></div><div class="missiongrid"><label>Total valence electrons<select id="me"><option>8</option><option>16</option><option>24</option><option>32</option></select></label><label>Total bond order around center<select id="mb"><option>2</option><option>3</option><option>4</option><option>5</option></select></label><label>Lone pairs on central atom<select id="ml"><option>0</option><option>1</option><option>2</option><option>3</option></select></label><label>Molecular geometry<select id="ms"><option>Linear</option><option>Bent</option><option>Trigonal planar</option><option>Trigonal pyramidal</option><option>Tetrahedral</option></select></label></div><button class="go" id="launch">Lock In Answer</button><button class="mini" id="exit">Back to lesson</button><div id="missionfb"></div></div>';$("#exit").onclick=lesson;$("#launch").onclick=()=>{let ok=+$("#me").value===m.e&&+$("#mb").value===m.b&&+$("#ml").value===m.l&&$("#ms").value===m.s;let f=$("#missionfb");if(ok){game.score+=100+game.streak*25;game.streak++;f.className="fb good";f.innerHTML="<b>Mission complete! +"+(100+(game.streak-1)*25)+" points</b><br>"+m.why+'<br><button class="go" id="nextmission">Next molecule</button>';$("#nextmission").onclick=()=>{game.i++;draw()}}else{game.lives--;game.streak=0;f.className="fb bad";if(game.lives<=0){f.innerHTML="<b>Reactor reset.</b> "+m.why+'<br><button class="go" id="retrymission">Try this molecule again</button>';$("#retrymission").onclick=()=>{game.lives=3;draw()}}else f.innerHTML="<b>Not stable yet.</b> Check electron counting, the central atom’s lone pairs, and VSEPR. "+game.lives+" ♥ remaining."}}}draw()}

let S={u:0,p:[],scores:{},final:0};try{const saved=localStorage.getItem("apchem");if(saved){const parsed=JSON.parse(saved);if(parsed&&Array.isArray(parsed.p))S=parsed}}catch(e){};let cur=0,Q=[],qi=0,right=0,miss=[];const $=s=>document.querySelector(s),sh=a=>[...a].sort(()=>Math.random()-.5);function save(){try{localStorage.setItem("apchem",JSON.stringify(S))}catch(e){}}function resetProgress(){S={u:0,p:[],scores:{},final:0};save();cur=0;lesson()}function nav(){let n=$("#nav");n.innerHTML="";M.forEach((m,i)=>{let b=document.createElement("button");b.textContent=(S.p.includes(i)?"✓ ":"")+m[0];b.disabled=i>S.u;b.className=i===cur?"on":"";b.onclick=()=>{cur=i;lesson()};n.append(b)});let f=document.createElement("button");f.textContent="Final Mastery Exam";f.disabled=S.u<M.length;f.onclick=finalExam;n.append(f);$("#bar").style.width=Math.round(S.p.length/M.length*100)+"%";$("#pt").textContent=S.p.length+" of "+M.length+" modules mastered"+(S.final?" • Best final: "+S.final+"%":"")}function lesson(){nav();$("#main").innerHTML="<h2>"+M[cur][0]+"</h2><div>"+M[cur][1]+"</div>"+(typeof G!=="undefined"&&G[cur]?G[cur]:"")+"<button class=go id=q>Take rotating mastery quiz</button>";if(typeof initGame==="function"&&typeof G!=="undefined"&&G[cur])initGame(cur);$("#q").onclick=()=>start(cur)}function make(pool,n){return sh(pool).slice(0,n).map(x=>({q:x[0],correct:x[1],opts:sh(x.slice(1,5)),explain:x[5]||""}))}function explainChoice(x,o){let base=x.explain||("The correct answer is “"+x.correct+"” because it matches the chemistry rule taught in this lesson.");let wrong=o===x.correct?"":("<br><br><b>Why your choice was not correct:</b> “"+o+"” does not match that rule in this situation. Compare it directly with “"+x.correct+"” and the lesson example.");return base+wrong}function start(i){Q=make(M[i][2],5);qi=0;right=0;miss=[];show()}function show(){let x=Q[qi];$("#main").innerHTML="<span class=pill>Question "+(qi+1)+" / "+Q.length+"</span><h2>"+x.q+"</h2><div id=choices></div><p class=mut>Question selection and answer order rotate every attempt.</p>";x.opts.forEach(o=>{let b=document.createElement("button");b.className="choice";b.textContent=o;b.onclick=()=>answer(o,x);$("#choices").append(b)})}function answer(o,x){let ok=o===x.correct;if(ok)right++;else miss.push(x);document.querySelectorAll(".choice").forEach(b=>b.disabled=true);let d=document.createElement("div");d.className="fb "+(ok?"good":"bad");d.innerHTML=(ok?'<div class="reaction-art correct"><img class="reaction-image" src="/assets/success.svg" alt="Scientist in a lab coat and goggles holding a bubbling beaker and giving a thumbs-up"><div><div class="lab-label">Experiment successful</div><b>Correct! Great chemistry.</b><br>':'<div class="reaction-art wrong"><img class="reaction-image" src="/assets/spill.svg" alt="A tipped laboratory beaker with blue fluid spilling onto the floor"><div><div class="lab-label">Lab spill — learn from it</div><b>Not quite. The beaker tipped!</b><br>The correct answer is <b>'+x.correct+'</b>.<br>')+explainChoice(x,o)+"</div></div>";$("#main").append(d);let b=document.createElement("button");b.className="go";b.textContent=qi<Q.length-1?"Next question":"See result";b.onclick=()=>{qi++;qi<Q.length?show():result()};$("#main").append(b)}function result(){let sc=Math.round(right/Q.length*100),pass=sc>=80;S.scores[cur]=Math.max(S.scores[cur]||0,sc);if(pass){if(!S.p.includes(cur))S.p.push(cur);S.u=Math.max(S.u,cur+1)}save();$("#main").innerHTML="<div class=big>"+sc+"%</div><h2>"+(pass?"Module mastered!":"Reteaching required")+"</h2><p>"+(pass?"Next section unlocked.":"You need 80%. Read the lesson again, then a fresh set of questions will be generated.")+"</p><button class=go id=next>"+(pass?"Continue":"Review lesson")+"</button>";$("#next").onclick=()=>{if(pass)cur=Math.min(cur+1,M.length-1);lesson()};nav()}function finalExam(){let all=[];M.forEach((m,i)=>sh(m[2]).slice(0,2).forEach(q=>all.push([...q,"Module "+(i+1)+": "+m[0]])));Q=sh(all).map(x=>({q:x[0],correct:x[1],opts:sh(x.slice(1,5)),topic:x[5]}));qi=0;right=0;miss=[];showFinal()}function showFinal(){let x=Q[qi];$("#main").innerHTML="<span class=pill>Final Exam • "+(qi+1)+" / "+Q.length+"</span><h2>"+x.q+"</h2><div id=choices></div>";x.opts.forEach(o=>{let b=document.createElement("button");b.className="choice";b.textContent=o;b.onclick=()=>{if(o===x.correct)right++;else miss.push(x);qi++;qi<Q.length?showFinal():finalResult()};$("#choices").append(b)})}function finalResult(){let sc=Math.round(right/Q.length*100);S.final=Math.max(S.final||0,sc);save();let weak=[...new Set(miss.map(x=>x.topic))];$("#main").innerHTML="<div class=big>"+sc+"%</div><h2>"+(sc>=90?"A-level mastery achieved!":"Targeted review needed")+"</h2><p>"+(sc>=90?"You reached the 90% mastery target. Great work.":"Review these areas before taking a newly randomized final exam: <b>"+weak.join(", ")+"</b>")+"</p><button class=go id=re>"+(sc>=90?"Take another version":"Review weak areas")+"</button>";$("#re").onclick=()=>{if(sc>=90)finalExam();else{let k=M.findIndex(m=>weak[0]&&weak[0].includes(m[0]));cur=k<0?0:k;lesson()}};nav()}lesson();const mb=$("#missionBtn");if(mb)mb.onclick=missionGame;const sb=$("#startBtn");if(sb)sb.onclick=()=>{cur=0;lesson();$("#main").scrollIntoView({behavior:"smooth",block:"start"})};const ss=$("#startupStatus");if(ss)ss.textContent="Ready — choose Start Lesson 1 or Molecule Mission.";const rb=$("#resetBtn");if(rb)rb.onclick=()=>{if(confirm("Reset all lesson progress on this device?"))resetProgress()};
const HELP=[
{keys:["ion","cation","anion"],a:"An ion is an atom or group of atoms with an electrical charge. If it loses electrons it becomes positive (a cation). If it gains electrons it becomes negative (an anion). Think: opposite charges attract."},
{keys:["lattice","lattice energy"],a:"A crystal lattice is an organized 3-D arrangement of ions. Lattice energy tells us how strongly those positive and negative ions hold onto each other. Bigger charges and smaller ion-to-ion distance usually mean stronger attraction."},
{keys:["covalent","covalent bond"],a:"A covalent bond is atoms sharing electrons. Instead of one atom fully giving an electron to another, the atoms share electron density between them."},
{keys:["metallic","metal"],a:"Metal atoms share a mobile 'sea' of electrons. Because those electrons can move, metals conduct electricity and heat well."},
{keys:["sigma","sigma bond"],a:"A sigma (σ) bond is the first bond between two atoms. The orbitals overlap head-on. Every single bond is one sigma bond."},
{keys:["pi","pi bond"],a:"A pi (π) bond is an extra bond formed by sideways orbital overlap. A double bond has one sigma plus one pi; a triple has one sigma plus two pi bonds."},
{keys:["bond order"],a:"Bond order is a simple way to count how many bonds connect two atoms: single = 1, double = 2, triple = 3. Higher bond order usually means a shorter, stronger bond."},
{keys:["potential energy","energy curve","bond length"],a:"Imagine two atoms approaching. Far apart, they barely interact. Closer, attraction lowers energy. At the best distance the energy is lowest—that is the equilibrium bond length. Push them too close and repulsion shoots up."},
{keys:["dissociation","dissociate"],a:"Dissociation means an ionic compound separates into its ions in water. NaCl becomes Na⁺ and Cl⁻. Those mobile charged particles are why the solution can conduct electricity."},
{keys:["conduct","conductivity","electricity"],a:"To conduct electricity, charged particles must be able to move. Metals have mobile electrons. Salt water has mobile ions. In solid salt the ions are charged but locked in place, so it conducts poorly."},
{keys:["lewis","lewis structure","valence"],a:"A Lewis structure is an electron bookkeeping picture. Start by counting valence electrons, connect the atoms, fill outer atoms' octets, then check the central atom and the total electron count."},
{keys:["octet"],a:"The octet rule is the useful idea that many main-group atoms become more stable with 8 electrons around them. It is a rule of thumb, not an absolute law—there are important exceptions."},
{keys:["resonance"],a:"Resonance means one Lewis drawing is not enough to show where electrons really are. We draw multiple valid electron arrangements; the real molecule is a blend, called a resonance hybrid."},
{keys:["formal charge"],a:"Formal charge is a bookkeeping tool for comparing Lewis structures: valence electrons − nonbonding electrons − half the bonding electrons. Usually we prefer reasonable structures with smaller formal charges."},
{keys:["vsepr","geometry","shape","lone pair"],a:"VSEPR says electron groups repel each other and spread out as much as possible. Count bonds around the central atom as electron domains (single/double/triple each count as one) and count lone pairs too. Those domains determine the 3-D shape."},
{keys:["tetrahedral"],a:"Tetrahedral means four electron domains spread into 3-D space, about 109.5° apart. CH₄ is the classic example."},
{keys:["trigonal pyramidal"],a:"Trigonal pyramidal usually means four electron domains: three bonds plus one lone pair, AX₃E. NH₃ is the classic example."},
{keys:["bent"],a:"Bent describes a molecule where the bonded atoms form an angle instead of a straight line. H₂O is bent because oxygen has two bonds and two lone pairs."}
];
HELP.push({keys:["intermolecular","dispersion","hydrogen bond","dipole"],a:"Intermolecular forces are attractions between separate molecules. All particles have dispersion forces; polar molecules can have dipole attractions; H bonded to N, O, or F can create hydrogen bonding."},{keys:["phase","solid","liquid","gas","boiling","melting"],a:"States of matter differ mostly in how particles move and how close they are. Solids are organized, liquids stay close but flow, and gases spread far apart. Phase changes involve changing particle attractions."},{keys:["ideal gas","pv=nrt","pressure","volume","kelvin"],a:"For gases, pressure comes from particles hitting container walls. PV=nRT connects pressure, volume, amount, and kelvin temperature. Smaller volume usually means higher pressure if the other conditions stay fixed."},{keys:["solution","solute","solvent","solubility"],a:"A solution is a uniform mixture. The solvent does the dissolving; the solute gets dissolved. A helpful first rule is like dissolves like: polar with polar, nonpolar with nonpolar."},{keys:["stoichiometry","balanced equation","coefficient","mole ratio"],a:"Stoichiometry treats a balanced chemical equation like a recipe. Balance with coefficients, then use those coefficients as mole ratios to calculate amounts."});HELP.push({keys:["spectator","net ionic","precipitate","precipitation"],a:"A net ionic equation removes spectator ions and shows only the particles that actually change. A precipitate is an insoluble solid that forms from ions in solution."},{keys:["acid","base","ph","hydronium"],a:"A simple acid–base picture: acids donate H⁺ and bases accept H⁺. Lower pH means more hydronium. Each pH unit is a tenfold change in hydronium concentration."},{keys:["enthalpy","exothermic","endothermic","thermochemistry"],a:"Thermochemistry tracks heat. Exothermic means heat leaves the chemical system (ΔH negative). Endothermic means the system absorbs heat (ΔH positive). Breaking bonds costs energy; forming bonds releases it."},{keys:["kinetics","rate","activation energy","catalyst"],a:"Kinetics is reaction speed. Particles need successful collisions and enough energy to cross the activation-energy barrier. Catalysts lower that barrier, giving an easier pathway."},{keys:["equilibrium","le chatelier","reversible"],a:"At dynamic equilibrium, forward and reverse reactions are both still happening at equal rates. Concentrations stay constant but do not have to be equal."},{keys:["oxidation","reduction","redox","anode","cathode","electrochemistry"],a:"Redox means electrons move. OIL RIG: Oxidation Is Loss of electrons; Reduction Is Gain. Oxidation happens at the anode and reduction at the cathode."});const SIMPLE=[
"Ionic bonding: positive and negative ions attract like opposite magnetic poles. Stronger charges and shorter distance mean stronger attraction.",
"Metallic vs covalent: metals have mobile shared electrons; covalent bonds hold specific atoms together by sharing electrons.",
"Bond order: single, double, triple means 1, 2, 3. More bonding generally makes the bond shorter and stronger.",
"Potential energy: atoms have a 'just right' distance where the bond is most stable—not too far and not too close.",
"Conductivity: ask whether charged particles exist and whether they are free to move.",
"Lewis structures are electron bookkeeping drawings. Count electrons first, then make sure you used the right total.",
"Resonance means electrons are spread out in a way one Lewis drawing cannot fully show.",
"VSEPR predicts 3-D shape by letting electron groups spread apart to reduce repulsion."
];
function helperReply(q){let s=q.toLowerCase().trim();if(!s)return "Ask me a chemistry question, even if it feels basic.";let hit=HELP.find(x=>x.keys.some(k=>s.includes(k)));if(hit)return hit.a;if(s.includes("charge")&&s.includes("distance"))return "For ionic attraction, charge strengthens the pull and distance weakens it. Try Lattice Lab: increase charge, then move the ions farther apart and watch the relative attraction number.";return "That is a good question. I can currently help with the concepts in this unit: ions, lattice energy, metallic/covalent bonding, sigma and pi bonds, bond order, potential energy, conductivity, Lewis structures, resonance, formal charge, octets, and VSEPR. Try asking with one of those terms."}
function addHelp(text,who="bot"){let d=document.createElement("div");d.className="msg "+who;d.textContent=text;$("#helperChat").append(d);$("#helperChat").scrollTop=$("#helperChat").scrollHeight}
function initHelper(){let launch=$("#helperLaunch"),box=$("#helper"),close=$("#helperClose"),form=$("#helperForm"),input=$("#helperInput");if(!launch||!box)return;launch.onclick=()=>{box.hidden=false;if(!$("#helperChat").children.length)addHelp("Hi! I’m Atom. Ask me anything about what you’re learning. I’ll keep it simple.");input.focus()};close.onclick=()=>box.hidden=true;form.onsubmit=e=>{e.preventDefault();let q=input.value.trim();if(!q)return;addHelp(q,"student");addHelp(helperReply(q));input.value=""};document.querySelectorAll("[data-help]").forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>{let type=b.dataset.help;if(type==="simple")addHelp(SIMPLE[cur]||"Focus on the main idea first, then use the example and simulator.");if(type==="example")addHelp(["NaCl is a simple ionic example: Na⁺ attracts Cl⁻.","Copper is metallic: mobile electrons help it conduct.","C=C is a double bond: 1σ + 1π.","Think of two magnets: there is a stable spacing, but pushing particles too close creates strong repulsion.","Salt water conducts because Na⁺ and Cl⁻ can move.","CO₂: count 16 valence electrons, then build O=C=O.","Ozone is a classic resonance example: one Lewis drawing alone is incomplete.","H₂O has four electron domains around O—two bonds and two lone pairs—so its molecular shape is bent."][cur]||"Try the lesson example.");if(type==="word")addHelp("Tell me the exact chemistry word that is confusing—for example: ion, lattice energy, sigma bond, resonance, formal charge, or VSEPR.");if(type==="why")addHelp("This matters because AP Chemistry questions often ask you to explain a property from particle-level structure—not just memorize a definition.")})}
initHelper();
